Here's a possible implementation of the C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing text in a reduced font style and returns a `std::string` containing the same text in HTML format:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string convertReducedFontToHTML(const std::string& inputText) {
    // Define regular expression pattern to match reduced font styles
    std::regex reducedFontPattern(R"(\\u([1-9a-fA-F]{4})|\\xc2\\x28([1-9a-fA-F]{2})\x29)");

    // Compile regular expression pattern
    std::regex reducedFontRegex(reducedFontPattern);

    // Create output string to store converted text
    std::string outputText;

    // Iterate over matched substrings and replace them with HTML tags
    std::smatch match;
    while (std::regex_search(inputText, match, reducedFontRegex)) {
        std::string reducedFontCode = match.str(1);
        if (reducedFontCode.size() == 4) {
            // Matched 4-digit Unicode code point
            int unicodeCodePoint = std::stoi(reducedFontCode, nullptr, 16);
            outputText += "<span style='font-size: 80%'>";
            outputText += static_cast<char>(unicodeCodePoint);
            outputText += "</span>";
        } else {
            // Matched 2-digit hexadecimal Unicode code point
            int unicodeCodePoint = std::stoi(match.str(2), nullptr, 16);
            outputText += "<span style='font-size: 80%'>";
            outputText += static_cast<char>(0x20AC); // Replace with the actual Euro symbol code point
            outputText += "</span>";
        }
        inputText = match.suffix();
    }

    return outputText;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression pattern to match reduced font styles represented as Unicode code points (`\uXXXX` or `\u00XX`). The function then iterates over the matched substrings, replaces them with HTML `<span>` tags with a smaller font size (`font-size: 80%`), and appends the resulting HTML text to a new output string.

Note that this implementation assumes that the reduced font styles are represented as Unicode code points, and that the Euro symbol (`\u20AC`) is used as an example. You may need to adjust the regular expression pattern and the replacement logic to fit your specific use case. Also, this implementation does not handle nested reduced font styles or other complex text formatting, but it should work for simple cases.

